#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *

#heading(level: 2)[Tensor Algebras] <sec:tensor-algebras>

We have already made frequent use of the fact that if $M$ and $N$ are left
$R G$-modules, then $M times.o_R N$ is a left $R G$-module, under the diagonal
action of $G$. In this section we extend the scope of this construction to
define actions of $G$ on tensor algebras, and on exterior and symmetric
algebras. In this context we shall define the Adams operators on the ring of
virtual characters of $G$, and develop some of their basic properties. We
finally present a result of Mackey on symmetric and antisymmetric squares of
induced modules, relating the tensor constructions to the results of
§@sec:induced-modules.

#heading(level: 3)[Tensor Algebras] <ss:tensor-symmetric-exterior-algebras>

Throughout this subsection, $R$ denotes a commutative ring, $G$ a group (not
necessarily finite), and $M$ a left $R G$-module. Unless otherwise stated,
$times.o$ means $times.o_R$. For each positive integer $n$, let
$ times.o^n M=M times.o dots.c times.o M quad (n "factors"). $
Then $times.o^n M$ is a left $R G$-module on which $G$ acts diagonally:
$
  g(m_1 times.o dots.c times.o m_n)=g m_1 times.o dots.c times.o g m_n,
  g in G,m_i in M.
$
We interpret $times.o^0 M$ as the $R G$-module $R$, on which $G$ acts trivially.
In much of the discussion to follow, the $R$-homomorphisms to be defined are
automatically $R G$-homomorphisms, and the group $G$ plays only a secondary role
in the considerations.

There is an obvious homomorphism
$ (times.o^k M) times.o (times.o^n M) -> times.o^(k+n) M, $
which we regard as multiplication of tensors, and denote by $times.o$. We then
put
$
  upright(bold(T))(M)=product.co_(n=0)^infinity times.o^n M
  =R plus.o M plus.o (M times.o M) plus.o dots.c,
$
#source(331)
and call $upright(bold(T))(M)$ the _tensor algebra_#idx("tensor algebra") of
$M$. Clearly $upright(bold(T))(M)$ is an associative noncommutative $R$-algebra,
and is also a left $R G$-module.

In case $M=plus.o_(i in I) R m_i$ is $R$-free, the elements
$ {m_(i_1) times.o dots.c times.o m_(i_n):i_1,dots,i_n in I} $
form a free $R$-basis for $times.o^n M$. (We have previously used the fact that
if $N=plus.o R n_j$ is also $R$-free, then ${m_i times.o n_j}$ forms a free
$R$-basis for $M times.o N$.) Further, if the index set $I$ is finite, then
$times.o^n M$ affords the matrix representation
$
  g -> upright(bold(M))(g) times.o dots.c times.o
  upright(bold(M))(g) quad (n "factors"),g in G,
$
where $upright(bold(M))$ is the matrix representation of $G$ afforded by $M$. If
$mu$ is the $R$-character afforded by $M$, then the $R$-character afforded by
$times.o^n M$ is the $n$-th power $mu^n$ of $mu$.

In general, we remark that $upright(bold(T))(M)$ is a _graded_ $R$-algebra#idx(
  "graded algebra",
), consisting of the ring $R$ at degree zero, and the $R$-module $times.o^n M$
at degree $n$, for $n>=1$. Each $R G$-homomorphism $M -> N$ gives rise to an
$R$-algebra homomorphism $upright(bold(T))(M) -> upright(bold(T))(N)$, which is
also a homomorphism of $R G$-modules. Finally, we note that
$
  g(t plus.minus t')=g t plus.minus g t',
  g(t times.o t')=g t times.o g t',g in G,t,t' in upright(bold(T))(M).
$

We next define the _symmetric algebra_#idx("symmetric algebra")
$upright(bold(S))(M)$ of a left $R G$-module $M$. The elements of
$upright(bold(S))(M)$ are finite sums of the form $sum m_1 or dots.c or m_r$,
$m_i in M$, with the property that
$ m_1 or dots.c or m_r=m_(1') or dots.c or m_(r'), $
for any permutation ${1,2,dots,r} -> {1',2',dots,r'}$. In order to construct
$upright(bold(S))(M)$, let $J$ be the two-sided ideal in the tensor algebra
$upright(bold(T))(M)$ generated by ${m times.o m'-m' times.o m:m,m' in M}$, and
set
$
  upright(bold(S))(M)=upright(bold(T))(M) slash J
  =product.co_(n=0)^infinity (times.o^n M) slash (J inter times.o^n M).
$
For each $x in G$, we have
$ x(m times.o m'-m' times.o m)=x m times.o x m'-x m' times.o x m, $
and it follows that $J$ is an $R G$-submodule of $upright(bold(T))(M)$.
Therefore $upright(bold(S))(M)$ is a commutative graded $R$-algebra:
$ upright(bold(S))(M)=product.co_(r=0)^infinity upright(bold(S))^(r)(M), $
and each submodule $upright(bold(S))^(r)(M)$ is an $R G$-submodule, whose
elements are called
#source(332)
_symmetric tensors of degree_ $r$#idx("symmetric tensor"). The elements of
$upright(bold(S))^(r)(M)$ have the form
$ sum m_1 or dots.c or m_r,m_i in M, $
where $m_1 or dots.c or m_r=(m_1 times.o dots.c times.o m_r)+J$. We shall also
use the notations
$
  M or M=upright(bold(S))^2(M),
  underbrace(M or dots.c or M, r)=upright(bold(S))^(r)(M).
$
In the special case where $M=plus.o_(i in I) R m_i$ is $R$-free, with $R$-basis
${m_i}_(i in I)$ for some ordered set of indices $I$, the elements
$ {m_(i_1) or dots.c or m_(i_r):i_1<=i_2<=dots<=i_r,i_k in I}, $
form a free $R$-basis for the symmetric tensors $upright(bold(S))^(r)(M)$ of
degree $r$. As in the case of the tensor algebra, each $R G$-homomorphism
$M -> N$ gives rise to an $R G$-homomorphism
$upright(bold(S))(M) -> upright(bold(S))(N)$, such that
$upright(bold(S))^(r)(M) -> upright(bold(S))^(r)(N)$, $r=0,1,2,dots$. We leave
it to the reader to check that there is an $R G$-isomorphism
$
  upright(bold(S))(M plus.o N) tilde.equiv
  upright(bold(S))(M) times.o_R upright(bold(S))(N),
$
<eq:symmetric-algebra-direct-sum>
and
$
  upright(bold(S))^(r)(M plus.o N) tilde.equiv
  product.co_(k=0)^r {
    upright(bold(S))^(k)(M) times.o_R upright(bold(S))^(r-k)(N)
  }.
$
We note that @eq:symmetric-algebra-direct-sum is also an isomorphism of
commutative $R$-algebras.

Another important construction involving $upright(bold(T))(M)$ leads to the
_exterior algebra_#idx("exterior algebra") $and(M)$ of an $R G$-module $M$. The
elements of $and(M)$ are finite sums of the form $sum m_1 and dots.c and m_r$,
$m_i in M$, with
$ m_1 and dots.c and m_r=epsilon(m_(1') and dots.c and m_(r')), $
where $epsilon$ is the sign of the permutation $1 -> 1',dots,r -> r'$. To
construct $and(M)$, let $I$ be the two-sided ideal in $upright(bold(T))(M)$
generated by the elements ${m times.o m:m in M}$. Then
$ I=plus.o_(r=0)^infinity (I inter times.o^r M), $
and we may set
$
  and(M)=upright(bold(T))(M) slash I
  =product.co_(r=0)^infinity (times.o^r M) slash (I inter times.o^r M).
$
We shall write $m and m'$ to denote the image in $and(M)$ of
$m times.o m' in upright(bold(T))(M)$. In
#source(333)
$and(M)$, we have
$ m and m=0,m and n=-n and m,m,n in M. $
It is clear that the $R$-module $and(M)$ is a graded $R$-algebra, and is an
$R G$-module. Moreover, the component
$and^(r)(M)=times.o^r M slash (I inter times.o^r M)$ is an $R G$-submodule,
called the module of _skew-symmetric tensors_#idx("skew-symmetric tensor") of
degree $r$. We shall often use the notation $M and M$ instead of $and^2(M)$.

In case $M=plus.o_(i in I) R m_i$ is $R$-free with ordered $R$-basis
${m_i}_(i in I)$, an $R$-basis of $and^(r)(M)$ is given by
$ {m_(i_1) and m_(i_2) and dots.c and m_(i_r):i_1<i_2<dots<i_r,i_k in I}. $
In particular, if $M=plus.o_(i=1)^n R m_i$, then $and^(r)(M)=0$ for $r>n$, while
for $r<=n$, the module $and^(r)(M)$ has a basis containing $binom(n, r)$
elements.

We remind the reader of the interpretation of $det f$, for a map
$f in End_(R)(M)$, with $M=plus.o_1^n R m_i$ as above. In this case
$ and^(n)(M)=R(m_1 and dots.c and m_n), $
and it follows that there exists a well-defined element $det f in R$ such that
$ f^* lr((m_1 and dots.c and m_n))=(det f)(m_1 and dots.c and m_n), $
where $f^*:and^(n)(M) -> and^(n)(M)$ is the $R$-linear map induced by $f$. (To
be explicit, $f^* lr((m_1 and dots.c and m_n))=f(m_1) and dots.c and
f(m_n)$.) If $f,g in End_(R)(M)$, then from the above definition we have
$ det f g=(det f)(det g). $
Returning to the general case, we note first that each $R G$-homomorphism
$M -> N$ gives rise to an $R G$-homomorphism $and(M) -> and(N)$, which maps
$and^(r)(M)$ into $and^(r)(N)$, for $r=0,1,2,dots$. Next, we observe that there
is a isomorphism of left $R G$-modules
$ and(M plus.o N) tilde.equiv and(M) times.o_R and(N), $
<eq:exterior-algebra-direct-sum>
with
$
  and^(r)(M plus.o N) tilde.equiv
  product.co_(k=0)^r {and^(k)(M) times.o_R and^(r-k)(N)}.
$
The map in @eq:exterior-algebra-direct-sum is not an isomorphism of
$R$-algebras, however, because the algebras involved are anticommutative. We may
define an $R$-algebra structure on $and(M) times.o_R and(N)$ by setting
$ (a times.o b)(a' times.o b')=(-1)^(j k) a a' times.o b b', $
#source(334)
whenever $b in and^(j)(N)$, $a' in and^(k)(M)$, and $a a'$, $b b'$ denote
products in the exterior algebras $and(M)$ and $and(N)$, respectively. With this
definition, the $R$-isomorphism in @eq:exterior-algebra-direct-sum becomes an
isomorphism of $R$-algebras. (For further details see Swan (@bib:Swan1968,
Ch.~8, Prop.~8.1).)

The algebras $upright(bold(T))(M)$, $upright(bold(S))(M)$ and $and(M)$ can all
be characterized by universal mapping properties. For example, let $M$ be an
$R$-module, and let
$ f:underbrace(M times dots.c times M, r) -> W $
be a symmetric $R$-multilinear map into an $R$-module $W$. Then there exists a
unique $R$-linear map $f':upright(bold(S))^(r)(M) -> W$ such that
$ f'(m_1 or dots.c or m_r)=f(m_1,dots,m_r),m_i in M. $

Finally, we point out a connection between the modules
$upright(bold(S))^(r)(M)$, $and^(r)(M)$ and representations of the symmetric
group. The symmetric group $S_r$ acts on $times.o^r M$ as follows. For
$sigma in S_r$, there is a unique $R$-endomorphism of $times.o^r M$, called a
_symmetry operator_#idx(
  "symmetry operator",
), defined by
$
  m_1 times.o dots.c times.o m_r -> sigma(m_1 times.o dots.c times.o m_r)
  =m_(sigma^(-1)(1)) times.o dots.c times.o m_(sigma^(-1)(r)).
$
The permutation $sigma$ acts on the places in which the elements in the product
$m_1 times.o dots.c times.o m_r$ are located, and not on the elements $m in M$;
in other words, $sigma$ rearranges the order of elements in the product, keeping
the same factors. It is easily checked that if $sigma,tau in S_r$, then
$ sigma(tau t)=(sigma tau)t,t in times.o^r M, $
and that if $M$ is an $R G$-module, then the action of $sigma in S_r$ commutes
with the diagonal action of $G$ on $times.o^r M$.

#proposition[
  Let $r! in R^dot$, the group of units of $R$. Then $upright(bold(S))^(r)(M)$
  and $and^(r)(M)$ are isomorphic to submodules of $times.o^r M$:
  $
    upright(bold(S))^(r)(M) tilde.equiv
    {t in times.o^r M:sigma t=t,forall sigma in S_r},
  $
  and
  $
    and^(r)(M) tilde.equiv
    {t in times.o^r M:sigma t=epsilon(sigma)t,forall sigma in S_r},
  $
  where $epsilon(sigma)$ is the sign of the permutation $sigma$.
] <prop:symmetric-exterior-tensors-as-invariants>

#proof[
  We shall discuss only the first isomorphism, and leave the proof of the second
  one as an exercise. We first observe that $(r!)^(-1) sum_(sigma in S_r) sigma$
  projects
  #source(335)
  $times.o^r M$ onto the submodule consisting of all tensors $t$ such that
  $sigma t=t$ for all $sigma in S_r$. Now let
  $f:times.o^r M -> upright(bold(S))^(r)(M)$ be the natural map, so that
  $ f(m_1 times.o dots.c times.o m_r)=m_1 or dots.c or m_r,m_i in M. $
  By the universal mapping property for symmetric tensors, there is an
  $R$-homomorphism $g:upright(bold(S))^(r)(M) -> times.o^(r)(M)$ such that
  $
    g(m_1 or dots.c or m_r)=(r!)^(-1)
    sum_(sigma in S_r) sigma(m_1 times.o dots.c times.o m_r),m_i in M.
  $
  Then $f compose g=1$, hence $g$ is a monomorphism from
  $upright(bold(S))^(r)(M)$ onto the submodule of $times.o^r M$ defined in the
  statement of the proposition, as we wished to prove.
]

In case $K$ is a field of characteristic zero, one can define $K G$-submodules
of $times.o^r M$ corresponding to all irreducible $K$-representations of $S_r$.
These were used by Schur to determine the irreducible tensor representations of
$GL(M)$ (see Weyl~@bib:Weyl1946, @bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR §~67]).

#heading(level: 3)[
  Adams Operators on the Ring of Virtual Characters
] <ss:adams-operators-virtual-characters>

In this subsection, $"ch"(K G)$ denotes the ring of virtual characters of a
finite group $G$ over a field $K$ of characteristic~0 (see
§@ss:finite-group-character-orthogonality). We recall that a class function
$mu:G -> K$ lies in $"ch"(K G)$ if and only if $mu$ is a $ZZ$-linear combination
of characters afforded by f.g. $K G$-modules.

Let $M$ be a left $K G$-module affording the character $mu$, and let
$n=dim_K M$. For an element $x in G$, let ${xi_1,dots,xi_n}$ be the eigenvalues
of the matrix describing the action of $x$ on a $K$-basis for $M$. Then
$ mu(x)=xi_1+dots+xi_n, $
by definition. From §@ss:tensor-symmetric-exterior-algebras, the eigenvalues of
$x$ acting on the exterior power $and^(r)(M)$ are
$ {xi_(i_1) dots.c xi_(i_r):1<=i_1<dots<i_r<=n}. $
Thus $and^(r)(M)$ affords the character $mu^((r))$ of $G$ defined by
$ mu^((r)) lr((x))=sum_(1<=i_1<dots<i_r<=n) xi_(i_1) dots.c xi_(i_r). $
(Note that for $r>n$, $and^(r)(M)=0$ and $mu^((r))=0$.) The value of $mu^((r))$
at $x$ is the $r$-th elementary symmetric function $s_r lr((xi_1,dots,xi_n))$ of
the eigenvalues of $x$ on $M$. In this section we shall introduce certain
virtual characters ${psi_m lr((mu)):m=1,2,dots}$, which are related to
${mu^((r)):r=1,2,dots}$ in the same way that the power sums $sum_j xi_j^m$ are
related to the elementary symmetric functions $s_r lr((xi_1,dots,xi_n))$ of $n$
variables $xi_1,dots,xi_n$.

#source(336)
Let $bold(X)={X_1,dots,X_n}$ be a set of $n$ variables, and define the
_elementary symmetric functions_#idx("elementary symmetric functions")
${s_r lr((bold(X))):r=1,2,dots}$ by
$ s_1 lr((bold(X)))=X_1+dots+X_n, $
$ s_2 lr((bold(X)))=sum_(1<=i<j<=n) X_i X_j, $
$ dots.c $
$ s_n lr((bold(X)))=X_1 dots.c X_n. $
We set $s_m lr((bold(X)))=0$ for $m>n$. Define the _power sums_#idx(
  "power sums",
) $p_i lr((bold(X)))$ by
$ p_i lr((bold(X)))=X_1^i+dots+X_n^i,i=1,2,dots. $
Let us first establish _Newton's identities_ connecting the
${s_r lr((bold(X)))}$ and ${p_i lr((bold(X)))}$. We omit the symbol $bold(X)$
for brevity in the following calculations. Let $t$ be a variable, and let
$ f(t)=product_(j=1)^n (1+t X_j)=1+s_1 t+s_2 t^2+dots+s_n t^n. $
Then (using formal power series)
$
  frac(f'(t), f(t))=frac(d, d t) log f(t)
  =sum_(j=1)^n frac(X_j, 1+t X_j)
  =sum_(k=0)^infinity sum_(j=1)^n (-1)^k X_j^(k+1)t^k
  =sum_(k=0)^infinity (-1)^k p_(k+1)t^k.
$
This gives
$
  s_1+2s_2 t+dots+n s_n t^(n-1)
  ={p_1-p_2 t+p_3 t^2-dots}{1+s_1 t+dots+s_n t^n}.
$
Comparing coefficients of powers of $t^k$, we obtain _Newton's identities_#idx(
  "Newton's identities",
):
$
  cases(
    p_1=s_1,
    p_2=s_1 p_1-2s_2,
    p_3=s_1 p_2-s_2 p_1+3s_3,
    dots.c,
    p_(k+1)=s_1 p_k-s_2 p_(k-1)+dots+(-1)^(k-1)s_k p_1
    +(-1)^(k)(k+1)s_(k+1),
    dots.c,
  )
$ <eq:newton-power-sum-identities>
The last equation holds for all $k>=1$, if we interpret $s_m$ as~0 for $m>n$.

#source(337)
We now imitate this procedure, working in the ring
$ A="ch"(K G)[[t]], $
the ring of formal power series in a variable $t$ over the commutative ring
$"ch"(K G)$ of virtual characters. Let $M$ be a $K G$-module affording the
character $mu$, and let ${mu^((r)):r>=1}$ be defined as above. We now set
$ E_t lr((M))=sum_(r=0)^infinity mu^((r))t^r in A, $
where $mu^((0))=1_G$, the trivial character of $G$. Note that $E_t lr((M))$ is
thus a power series whose constant term~1 is the identity element of the
coefficient ring $"ch"(K G)$. It follows that $E_t lr((M))$ is a unit of the
power series ring $A$. We shall often write $E_t lr((mu))$ instead of
$E_t lr((M))$.

Let $N$ be another $K G$-module, affording the character $nu$ of $G$. By
@eq:exterior-algebra-direct-sum there is a $K G$-isomorphism
$
  and^(r)(M plus.o N) tilde.equiv
  product.co_(i+j=r) (and^i M) times.o_K (and^j N).
$
In terms of characters, this yields
$ (mu+nu)^((r))=sum_(i+j=r) mu^((i))nu^((j)). $
This formula is equivalent to the assertion
$ E_t lr((mu+nu))=E_t lr((mu)) E_t lr((nu)) $
<eq:exterior-character-series-additivity>
for any two characters $mu,nu$ of $G$. It follows that we can extend $E_t$
uniquely to a homomorphism from the additive group $"ch"(K G)$ into the
multiplicative group $A^dot$ of units of the power series ring $A$, by setting
$ E_t lr((mu-nu))=E_t lr((mu)) slash E_t lr((nu)) $
for any pair of characters $mu,nu$ of $G$. Note that
$E_t lr((-nu)) E_t lr((nu))=1$ for each $nu in "ch"(K G)$.

For real $x$ close to~1, we have
$ log x=log(1+(x-1))=(x-1)-(x-1)^2 slash 2+(x-1)^3 slash 3-dots. $
If we replace $x$ by an element $a in A$ with constant term~1, then the series
$ sum_(n=1)^infinity (-1)^(n-1)(a-1)^n slash n $
#source(338)
defines an element of $(QQ times.o_ZZ "ch"(K G))[[t]]$. We denote this element
by $log a$. Clearly
$ log a b=log a+log b $
whenever $a$ and $b$ are elements of $A$ with constant term~1. Moreover,
$ frac(d, d t)(log a)=a^(-1) frac(d a, d t), $
where $d slash d t$ denotes formal differentiation in this rational extension of
$A$. Since $a$ is a unit of $A$, its logarithmic derivative
$a^(-1) frac(d a, d t)$ still belongs to $A$.

We shall use these concepts to define the _Adams operators_#idx(
  "Adams operators",
)
$ psi_m:"ch"(K G) -> "ch"(K G),m=1,2,dots. $
Specifically, for each virtual character $mu in "ch"(K G)$, let
$psi_1 lr((mu)),psi_2 lr((mu)),dots$ be the uniquely determined coefficients of
$t,t^2,dots$, respectively, in the power series expansion of
$ -t frac(d, d t){log E_(-t) lr((mu))}. $
Thus, by definition,
$
  sum_(m=1)^infinity psi_m lr((mu))t^m
  =-t frac(d, d t){log E_(-t) lr((mu))}
  =frac(-t frac(d, d t){E_(-t) lr((mu))}, E_(-t) lr((mu))),
$
<eq:adams-logarithmic-derivative>
where
$
  E_(-t) lr((mu))=1-mu t+mu^((2))t^2-dots
  =sum_(r=0)^infinity (-1)^r mu^((r))t^r.
$
From the last expression in @eq:adams-logarithmic-derivative, we may deduce that
for $m>=1$, $psi_m lr((mu))$ is expressible as a polynomial in
$mu^((1)),mu^((2)),dots,mu^((m))$ with coefficients in $ZZ$. These polynomials
are defined recursively by the identity
$
  {1-mu^((1))t+mu^((2))t^2-dots}
  {psi_1 lr((mu))t+psi_2 lr((mu))t^2+dots}
  =mu^((1))t-2mu^((2))t^2+3mu^((3))t^3-dots.
$
Comparing coefficients of powers of $t$, we obtain
$
  cases(
    psi_1 lr((mu))=mu^((1))=mu,
    psi_2 lr((mu))=mu^((1))psi_1 lr((mu))-2mu^((2)),
    psi_3 lr((mu))=mu^((1))psi_2 lr((mu))
    -mu^((2))psi_1 lr((mu))+3mu^((3)),
    dots.c,
    psi_m lr((mu))=sum_(i=1)^(m-1) (-1)^(i-1)mu^((i))
    psi_(m-i) lr((mu))+(-1)^(m-1)m mu^((m)),
  )
$ <eq:adams-newton-recursion>

#source(339)
These formulas confirm our earlier statement that the Adams operators
$psi_i lr((mu))$ are given in terms of the characters $mu^((j))$ by the same
formulas @eq:newton-power-sum-identities (_Newton's identities_) which express
the power sums $p_i lr((X_1,dots,X_n))=sum_k X_k^i$ in terms of the elementary
symmetric functions $s_j lr((X_1,dots,X_n))$. This connection is strengthened by

#proposition[
  For each $x in G$ and each $mu in "ch"(K G)$, we have
  $ {psi_m lr((mu))}(x)=mu(x^m),m=1,2,dots. $
] <prop:adams-character-power-evaluation>

Before proving @prop:adams-character-power-evaluation, we first establish

#lemma[
  For all $mu,nu in "ch"(K G)$, and $m>=1$, we have
  $ psi_m lr((mu+nu))=psi_m lr((mu))+psi_m lr((nu)). $
] <lem:adams-additivity>

#proof[
  From @eq:adams-logarithmic-derivative, we have
  $
    sum_(m=1)^infinity psi_m lr((mu+nu))t^m
    =-t frac(d, d t){log E_(-t) lr((mu+nu))}.
  $
  Applying @eq:exterior-character-series-additivity to the right hand side, we
  obtain
  $
    -t frac(d, d t){log E_(-t) lr((mu+nu))}
    &=-t frac(d, d t){log E_(-t) lr((mu)) E_(-t) lr((nu))} \
    &=-t frac(d, d t){log E_(-t) lr((mu))}
    -t frac(d, d t){log E_(-t) lr((nu))}.
  $
  Upon expanding the summands using @eq:adams-logarithmic-derivative and
  comparing coefficients of powers of $t$, we obtain @lem:adams-additivity.
]

#proof(head: emph[Proof of @prop:adams-character-power-evaluation.])[
  By Lemma~@lem:adams-additivity, it suffices to prove
  @prop:adams-character-power-evaluation for a $K$-character $mu$ afforded by a
  $K G$-module $M$. As above, let $x in G$ and let ${xi_1,dots,xi_n}$ be the
  eigenvalues of $x$ on $M$. We have shown that
  $ mu^((m)) lr((x))=s_m lr((xi_1,dots,xi_n)),m>=1. $
  For each series $sum phi_m t^m in A$, we obtain a formal series in $K[[t]]$ by
  evaluation at $x$, namely
  $ (sum phi_m t^m)(x)=sum phi_m lr((x))t^m,x in G. $
  Then from the fact that ${mu^((m)) lr((x))}$ are given by the elementary
  symmetric functions in ${xi_1,dots,xi_n}$, we have
  $
    (E_t lr((mu)))(x)=sum_(m=0)^infinity mu^((m)) lr((x))t^m
    =product_(i=1)^n (1+xi_i t).
  $
  #source(340)
  Therefore
  $ (log E_(-t) lr((mu)))(x)=sum_(i=1)^n log(1-xi_i t), $
  and from @eq:adams-logarithmic-derivative we obtain
  $
    (sum_(m=1)^infinity psi_m lr((mu))t^m)(x)
    =-t frac(d, d t){log E_(-t) lr((mu))}(x)
    =sum_(i=1)^n frac(xi_i t, 1-xi_i t)
    =sum_(i=1)^n (sum_(m=1)^infinity xi_i^m t^m).
  $
  Comparing coefficients of $t^m$, we find that
  $ {psi_m lr((mu))}(x)=sum_(i=1)^n xi_i^m,m>=1. $
  However, ${xi_1^m,dots,xi_n^m}$ are precisely the eigenvalues of $x^m$ acting
  on the module $M$, so that
  $ {psi_m lr((mu))}(x)=mu(x^m),x in G, $
  and @prop:adams-character-power-evaluation is proved. (Compare this
  calculation with that which we used to establish
  @eq:newton-power-sum-identities.)
]

#corollary[
  The Adams operators ${psi_m}$ satisfy
  $ psi_m lr((mu nu))=psi_m lr((mu))psi_m lr((nu)) $
  and
  $ psi_m lr((psi_n lr((nu))))=psi_(m n) lr((nu)), $
  for all $m,n>=1$, and all $mu,nu in "ch"(K G)$. In particular, each Adams
  operator is an endomorphism of the commutative ring $"ch"(K G)$.
] <cor:adams-ring-endomorphisms>

#proof[
  The first two statements follow at once by evaluating both sides at $x in G$,
  and using @prop:adams-character-power-evaluation. The third statement follows
  from the first, and Lemma~@lem:adams-additivity.
]

The Adams operators will play an important role in our discussion of
$lambda$-rings.

#heading(level: 3)[
  Symmetric and Skew-Symmetric Squares of Induced Modules
] <ss:symmetric-skew-squares-induced-modules>

Throughout this section, $R$ denotes a commutative ring in which~2 is a unit,
that is, the equation $2x=1$ has a unique solution in $R$. Thus, in particular,
$2!=0$ in $R$. Let $G$ be a finite group, and $M$ a left $R G$-lattice (see
§@ss:contragredient-modules). Then
#source(341)
the inner tensor product $M times.o M$ is also an $R G$-lattice (here, and
throughout this subsection, $times.o$ always means $times.o_R$). By
Proposition~@prop:symmetric-exterior-tensors-as-invariants, the symmetric and
skew-symmetric tensors of degree~2 over $M$ can be identified with submodules of
$M times.o M$ as follows:
$ M or M={t in M times.o M:tau(t)=t}="symmetric tensors", $
#idx("symmetric tensor")
$ M and M={t in M times.o M:tau(t)=-t}="skew-symmetric tensors", $
#idx("skew-symmetric tensor")
where $tau$ is the symmetry operator corresponding to the transposition $(12)$.
Because of the hypothesis on $R$, we can write
$
  t=frac(1, 2)(t+tau(t))+frac(1, 2)(t-tau(t))
  in (M or M)+(M and M),
$
for all $t in M times.o M$. Moreover, $(M or M) inter (M and M)=0$, again by the
hypothesis on $R$. This shows that
$ M times.o M=(M or M) plus.o (M and M). $
This decomposition gives rise to a direct sum decomposition of
$Hom_(R G) lr((M^*,M))$, where $M^*$ is the $R G$-lattice which is the
contragredient of $M$ (see §@ss:contragredient-modules). By
@prop:invariants-induction-hom we have an $R$-isomorphism
$ Hom_(R G) lr((M^*,M)) tilde.equiv "inv"_G lr((M times.o M)). $
Using the above decomposition of $M times.o M$, we obtain
$
  Hom_(R G) lr((M^*,M)) tilde.equiv "inv"_G lr((M times.o M))
  ="inv"_G lr((M or M)) plus.o "inv"_G lr((M and M)).
$
Suppose now that $R$ is a field $K$, and define
$
  i_s lr((M^*,M))=dim_K lr(("inv"_G M or M)),
  i_a lr((M^*,M))=dim_K lr(("inv"_G M and M)).
$
We shall call $i_s lr((M^*,M))$ the _symmetric intertwining number_ of $M^*$ and
$M$, and $i_a lr((M^*,M))$ the _antisymmetric_ (or _skew-symmetric_)
_intertwining number_#idx("intertwining number"). Clearly,
$
  i(M^*,M)=dim_K Hom_(K G) lr((M^*,M))
  =i_s lr((M^*,M))+i_a lr((M^*,M)),
$
the first equality merely recalling the definition of $i(M^*,M)$ as in
@prop:character-scalar-products-multiplicities(iii).

We now introduce another important invariant of $M$, namely,
$ c(M)=i_s lr((M^*,M))-i_a lr((M^*,M)). $
If the field $K$ is algebraically closed and $M$ is a simple $K G$-module, it is
easily shown that $i(M^*,M)=1$ or~0, so
$ c(M)=1,-1 "or" 0. $
#source(342)
In Chapter~6 of Volume~II below, we shall prove a beautiful result of Frobenius
and Schur, which describes the field of definition of $M$ (and of its character)
in terms of $c(M)$.

The next result summarizes some useful properties of the invariant $c(M)$. The
proof is left as an exercise (see Mackey~@bib:Mackey1953).

#proposition[
  Let $K$ be a field such that $char K!=2$. For each left $K G$-module $M$,
  define
  $ c(M)=i_s lr((M^*,M))-i_a lr((M^*,M)). $
  Then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$c(M plus.o N)=c(M)+c(N)$.#ed-note[
        The original also asserts $c(M times.o N)=c(M)c(N)$. For $K=CC$, let $M$
        afford a nontrivial linear character of the cyclic group of order~3, and
        put $N=M^*$. Then $c(M)=c(N)=0$, whereas $M times.o N$ is trivial and
        has invariant~1. Thus $c$ is additive but need not be multiplicative.
      ]],
    [Further, if $char K=0$ or $char K divides.not |G|$, then
      $ c(M^*)=c(M). $
    ],
    [If $char K=0$, there are a ring homomorphism $dim:"ch"(K G) -> ZZ$ and an
      additive-group homomorphism $c:"ch"(K G) -> ZZ$, defined by
      $
        dim M=dim_K lr((M)),
        c(M)=i_s lr((M^*,M))-i_a lr((M^*,M)),
      $
      for every f.g. left $K G$-module $M$.],
  )
] <prop:symmetric-intertwining-invariant-properties>

We now return to the general situation, with $R$ a commutative ring such that
$2 in R^dot$. Let $H<=G$, and let $L$ be a left $R H$-lattice. As we have seen
in §@ss:contragredient-modules, $L^G$ is an $R G$-lattice. The main result
(Mackey~@bib:Mackey1953) describes the structure of the symmetric square
$L^G or L^G$ of $L^G$, and the skew-symmetric square $L^G and L^G$ of $L^G$, in
terms of induced modules.

In order to state _Mackey's Theorem_#idx("Mackey's Theorem"), some notation is
needed. Let $H$ be a subgroup of the finite group $G$, and consider the
collection $H without G slash H$ of $(H,H)$-double cosets in $G$. Write
$ H without G slash H=H union frak(D)_1 union frak(D)_2, $
<eq:inverse-double-coset-partition>
where $frak(D)_1$ is the union of the self-inverse double cosets not equal to
$H$, and $frak(D)_2$ is the union of the sets ${d}$ of the form
$d=D union D^(-1)$, where $D$ is a double coset such that $D!=D^(-1)$. Keeping
this notation, we have:

#theorem(title: [Mackey~@bib:Mackey1953])[
  Let $R$ be any commutative ring in which~2 is a unit, let $H$ be a subgroup of
  $G$, and $L$ an $R H$-lattice. Then $L^G or L^G$ and
  #source(343)
  $L^G and L^G$ are direct sums of induced representations as follows:
  $
    L^G or L^G=(L or L)^G plus.o
    product.co_(d in frak(D)_1) M(d)^+ plus.o product.co_(d in frak(D)_2) N(d),
  $
  $
    L^G and L^G=(L and L)^G plus.o
    product.co_(d in frak(D)_1) M(d)^- plus.o product.co_(d in frak(D)_2) N(d).
  $
  The modules $M(d)^plus.minus$ and $N(d)$ are given thus:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [Let $d=D union D^(-1) in frak(D)_2$, and let $x,y in G$ be such that
      $x^(-1)y in D$. Then the induced module
      $
        {(attach(L, tl: x) times.o attach(L, tl: y))_(
          attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)
          )}^G
      $
      is independent of the choice of $x$ and $y$, and is denoted by $N(d)$.],
    [Let $d=D in frak(D)_1$, so $D$ is a double coset such that $D=D^(-1)$,
      $D!=H$. Let $x,y in G$ be such that $x^(-1)y in D$; then
      $x H y^(-1) inter y H x^(-1)!=emptyset$. Choose
      $z in (x H y^(-1) inter y H x^(-1))$, and set
      $ H_z=chevron.l attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y),z chevron.r, $
      a subgroup of $G$ containing $attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)$ as
      a normal subgroup of index~2.

      The $R(attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y))$-module
      $attach(L, tl: x) times.o attach(L, tl: y)$ can be extended to two
      $R H_z$-modules $L_(x,y,z)^plus.minus$, whose direct sum is
      $(attach(L, tl: x) times.o attach(L, tl: y))^(H_z)$. The modules
      $M(d)^plus.minus$ occurring in the decompositions of $L^G or L^G$ and
      $L^G and L^G$ are then defined by
      $
        M(d)^+="ind"_(H_z)^G L_(x,y,z)^+,
        M(d)^-="ind"_(H_z)^G L_(x,y,z)^-.
      $
      These modules are independent of the choices of $x,y,z$ (provided these
      are chosen so that $x^(-1)y in D$, and
      $z in x H y^(-1) inter y H x^(-1)$).],
  )
] <th:mackey-symmetric-skew-induced-squares>

#proof[
  The entire argument is based on the proof of the Tensor Product
  Theorem~@th:mackey-tensor-product. We have
  $
    L^G times.o L^G tilde.equiv
    (L \# L)^(G times G)|_(G_0),
  $
  where $G_0$ is the diagonal subgroup of $G times G$. From the Subgroup
  Theorem~@th:mackey-subgroup, we have
  $
    (L \# L)^(G times G)|_(G_0)
    =plus.o_D {
      (x times.o L times.o y times.o L)|_(
      (attach(H, tl: x) times attach(H, tl: y)) inter G_0
      )
    }^(G_0),
  $
  <eq:mackey-square-subgroup-decomposition>
  where the sum is taken over $(H,H)$-double cosets $D$ in $G$. The double
  cosets
  #source(344)
  $D$ correspond bijectively to the $(G_0,H times H)$-double cosets in
  $G times G$:
  $ H x^(-1)y H arrow.l.r G_0(x,y)(H times H). $
  The summand in @eq:mackey-square-subgroup-decomposition corresponding to $D$
  consists of all tensors of the form
  $
    sum_(g in G,l in L,l' in L) (g,g)(x times.o l times.o y times.o l')
    =sum g x times.o l times.o g y times.o l',g in G,l,l' in L.
  $
  The symmetry operator $tau:L^G times.o L^G -> L^G times.o L^G$, corresponding
  to the transposition $(12)$, is defined by:
  $
    tau:sum g x times.o l times.o g y times.o l'
    -> sum g y times.o l' times.o g x times.o l.
  $
  Note that $tau$ commutes with the action of all elements $(g,g) in G_0$. We
  recall from the beginning of §@ss:symmetric-skew-squares-induced-modules that
  $L^G or L^G$ is identified with the submodule consisting of all elements of
  $L^G times.o L^G$ fixed by $tau$, and $L^G and L^G$ with those elements mapped
  onto their negatives by $tau$.

  We now see that $(L \# L)^(G times G)|_(G_0)$ is the direct sum of
  $R G_0$-modules, which correspond as follows to the decomposition
  @eq:inverse-double-coset-partition of $H without G slash H$:
  $
    cases(
      (1 times.o L times.o 1 times.o L)^(G_0) arrow.l.r H,
      (x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(G_0) arrow.l.r D in frak(D)_1 comma
      D=D^(-1) comma x^(-1)y in D,
      (x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(G_0)
      +(y times.o L times.o x times.o L)^(G_0)
      arrow.l.r D union D^(-1) comma D!=D^(-1) comma x^(-1)y in D,
    )
  $ <eq:mackey-square-inversion-orbits>
  Note that in the last case, the double cosets in
  $G_0 without (G times G) slash (H times H)$ corresponding to $D$ and $D^(-1)$
  have representatives $(x,y)$ and $(y,x)$, respectively.

  Each of the submodules in @eq:mackey-square-inversion-orbits is mapped onto
  itself by $tau$, so that in order to prove the theorem, it is sufficient to
  determine the symmetric and skew-symmetric summands of the modules appearing
  in @eq:mackey-square-inversion-orbits.

  The first and third cases in @eq:mackey-square-inversion-orbits are easy to
  handle. For the first case, we leave it to the reader to verify that under the
  isomorphism $G_0 tilde.equiv G$, the module
  $(1 times.o L times.o 1 times.o L)^(G_0)$ corresponds to
  $(L or L)^G plus.o (L and L)^G$, with $(L or L)^G subset.eq L^G or L^G$ and
  $(L and L)^G subset.eq L^G and L^G$.

  For the third case, the symmetric and skew-symmetric summands of
  $(x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(G_0)
  +(y times.o L times.o x times.o L)^(G_0)$
  consist (respectively) of the elements
  $ {a+tau(a):a in (x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(G_0)} $
  and
  $ {a-tau(a):a in (x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(G_0)}. $
  #source(345)
  It is easily checked that the maps
  $ a -> a+tau(a),a -> a-tau(a),a in (x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(G_0), $
  are $R G_0$-isomorphisms. It follows that for each non-self-inverse double
  coset $D$, the third module listed in @eq:mackey-square-inversion-orbits
  contributes one summand to $L^G or L^G$ and one to $L^G and L^G$, and that
  both are $R G$-isomorphic to ${(attach(L, tl: x) times.o attach(L, tl: y))_(
    attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)
    )}^G$.

  We now consider the most difficult case, that of a double coset $D$ such that
  $D=D^(-1)$, $D!=H$. Let $x^(-1)y in D$; then $H x^(-1)y H=H y^(-1)x H$, and it
  follows that
  $ x H y^(-1) inter y H x^(-1)!=emptyset. $
  Choose $z in x H y^(-1) inter y H x^(-1)$. Then
  $
    attach((attach(H, tl: x)), tl: z)=attach(H, tl: y),
    attach((attach(H, tl: y)), tl: z)=attach(H, tl: x),
  $
  and hence
  $
    attach((attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)), tl: z)
    =attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y).
  $
  Moreover $z^2 in attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)$, so we may conclude
  that $attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)$ is a normal subgroup of index~2
  in $chevron.l attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y),z chevron.r$. We are
  going to prove that the $(attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y))$-module
  $x times.o L times.o y times.o L$ can be made into a module over the group
  $chevron.l attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y),z chevron.r$. Transferring
  the problem to the subgroup $G_0$ of $G times G$, we define subgroups
  $H_(x,y)$ and $H_(x,y,z)$ as follows:
  $
    attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y) arrow.l.r
    (attach(H, tl: x) times attach(H, tl: y)) inter G_0=H_(x,y),
  $
  $
    chevron.l attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y),z chevron.r arrow.l.r
    chevron.l
    (attach(H, tl: x) times attach(H, tl: y)) inter G_0,(z,z) chevron.r
    =H_(x,y,z).
  $
  We shall prove below that the action of $H_(x,y)$ on
  $x times.o L times.o y times.o L$ can be extended to an action of $H_(x,y,z)$,
  thereby making $x times.o L times.o y times.o L$ into an $R H_(x,y,z)$-module.
  Observe first that both of the elements $x^(-1)z y$ and $y^(-1)z x$ lie in
  $H$. Now define a linear map $Z$ on $x times.o L times.o y times.o L$ by
  $
    Z(x times.o l times.o y times.o l')
    =x times.o x^(-1)z y l' times.o y times.o y^(-1)z x l,forall l,l' in L.
  $
  Then
  $
    Z^2(x times.o l times.o y times.o l')
    &=x times.o (x^(-1)z y)(y^(-1)z x l) times.o y times.o
    (y^(-1)z x)(x^(-1)z y l') \
    &=x times.o x^(-1)z^2 x l times.o y times.o y^(-1)z^2 y l' \
    &=(z^2,z^2)(x times.o l times.o y times.o l').
  $
  It is easily checked that for $h in H_(x,y)$, the actions of
  $(z,z)h(z,z)^(-1)$ and $Z h Z^(-1)$ on $x times.o L times.o y times.o L$
  agree; this depends on the fact that
  $
    Z^(-1)(x times.o l times.o y times.o l')
    =x times.o x^(-1)z^(-1)y l' times.o y times.o y^(-1)z^(-1)x l.
  $
  #source(346)
  It now follows that the actions of $H_(x,y)$ and $Z$ on
  $x times.o L times.o y times.o L$ preserve the defining relations of the group
  $H_(x,y,z)$, so this larger group now acts on the module
  $x times.o L times.o y times.o L$, by letting $(z,z)$ act as $Z$.

  If $R$ were the field of complex numbers, we could now apply
  Corollary~@cor:gallagher-invariant-character in the irreducible case (or the
  following lemma in general) to deduce that the action of $H_(x,y)$ on
  $x times.o L times.o y times.o L$ could be extended in two ways to an action
  of $H_(x,y,z)$, thereby obtaining a pair of $R H_(x,y,z)$-modules $L^+$ and
  $L^-$ for which
  $
    "ind"_(H_(x,y))^(H_(x,y,z))(x times.o L times.o y times.o L)
    =L^+ plus.o L^-.
  $
  This would give the formula
  $
    "ind"_(H_(x,y))^(G_0)(x times.o L times.o y times.o L)
    =(L^+)^(G_0) plus.o (L^-)^(G_0).
  $
  We shall now establish these statements in the general case where $R$ is a
  ring (in which $2 in R^dot$), rather than $CC$. Further, as in the statement
  of the theorem, we shall identify $(L^+)^(G_0)$ with a direct summand of
  $L^G or L^G$, and $(L^-)^(G_0)$ with a direct summand of $L^G and L^G$, after
  transferring back from $G_0$ to $G$, from $H_(x,y,z)$ to $H_z$, and so on.

  The remainder of the proof hinges on the following lemma:

  #lemma[
    Let $R$ be a commutative ring such that $2 in R^dot$. Let $U$ be a normal
    subgroup of index~2 in a finite group $X$, and let
    $X=chevron.l U,x chevron.r$. Let $N$ be a left $R U$-module, and assume that
    the action of $U$ on $N$ can be extended to an action of $X$. Then
    $N^X=N^+ plus.o N^-$, where $N^+$ and $N^-$ are $R X$-modules such that
    $N^+|_U tilde.equiv N^-|_U tilde.equiv N$, and
    $
      N^+={1 times.o n+x times.o x^(-1)n:n in N},
      N^-={1 times.o n-x times.o x^(-1)n:n in N}.
    $
  ] <lem:index-two-extension-induced-splitting>

  The proof is deferred to the exercises. We can now complete the proof of
  @th:mackey-symmetric-skew-induced-squares. Using
  Lemma~@lem:index-two-extension-induced-splitting, we can write
  $ (x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(H_(x,y,z))=L^+ plus.o L^-, $
  where $L^+$ and $L^-$ are defined as in the lemma. It remains to prove that
  $(L^+)^(G_0)$ and $(L^-)^(G_0)$ are indeed the symmetric and skew-symmetric
  parts of $(x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(G_0)$. We can identify $L^+$ with
  the submodule of $(x times.o L times.o y times.o L)^(H_(x,y,z))$ consisting of
  all sums
  $
    sum {x times.o l times.o y times.o l'
      +(z,z)Z^(-1)(x times.o l times.o y times.o l')},l,l' in L.
  $
  Then $(L^+)^(G_0)$ consists of all finite sums
  $
    a & =sum_(g in G,l,l' in L)
        {g x times.o l times.o g y times.o l'
          +(g,g)(z,z)Z^(-1)(x times.o l times.o y times.o l')} \
      & =sum {g x times.o l times.o g y times.o l'
          +g z x times.o x^(-1)z^(-1)y l' times.o
          g z y times.o y^(-1)z^(-1)x l}.
  $
  #source(347)
  Therefore
  $
    tau a=sum {g y times.o l' times.o g x times.o l
      +g z y times.o y^(-1)z^(-1)x l times.o
      g z x times.o x^(-1)z^(-1)y l'}.
  $
  Using the fact that both $y^(-1)z x$ and $x^(-1)z y$ lie in $H$, it is easy to
  check that $tau a=a$. A similar calculation shows that for all elements
  $b in (L^-)^(G_0)$, we have $tau b=-b$.

  We can now transfer to $G$ by using the isomorphism $G_0 tilde.equiv G$. The
  modules $L^+$ and $L^-$ correspond to $R H_z$-modules $L_(x,y,z)^+$ and
  $L_(x,y,z)^-$, respectively, whose direct sum is
  $(attach(L, tl: x) times.o attach(L, tl: y))^(H_z)$. The modules
  $"ind"_(H_z)^G L_(x,y,z)^+$ and $"ind"_(H_z)^G L_(x,y,z)^-$ satisfy the
  requirements of the theorem, and the proof of
  @th:mackey-symmetric-skew-induced-squares is complete.
]

It is worthwhile to restate Theorem~@th:mackey-symmetric-skew-induced-squares as
it applies to a linear character of $H$, taken in the complex field $CC$.

#theorem[
  Let $H<=G$, and let $lambda$ be a linear $CC$-character of $H$. Let
  $lambda^G or lambda^G$ and $lambda^G and lambda^G$ denote the characters
  afforded by the symmetric and skew-symmetric parts of the tensor square of the
  module affording $lambda^G$. Let
  $ H without G slash H=H union frak(D)_1 union frak(D)_2 $
  as in @eq:inverse-double-coset-partition. Then
  $
    lambda^G or lambda^G=(lambda^2)^G
    +sum_(d in frak(D)_1) mu(d)^++sum_(d in frak(D)_2) nu(d),
  $
  $
    lambda^G and lambda^G=sum_(d in frak(D)_1) mu(d)^-
    +sum_(d in frak(D)_2) nu(d),
  $
  where $mu(d)^+$, $mu(d)^-$ and $nu(d)$ are induced characters defined as
  follows:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [For $d in frak(D)_2$, let $d=D union D^(-1)$ and let $x^(-1)y in D$, where
      $x,y in G$. Then
      $
        nu(d)="ind"_(attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y))^G
        attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y),
      $
      and $nu(d)$ is independent of the choice of $x$ and $y$.],
    [Now let $d={D}$ be an element of $frak(D)_1$, where $D=D^(-1)$. Let
      $z in x H y^(-1) inter y H x^(-1)$, and
      $H_z=chevron.l attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y),z chevron.r$ as in
      @th:mackey-symmetric-skew-induced-squares. Then
      $(attach(lambda, tl: x))(z^2)=(attach(lambda, tl: y))(z^2)$, and
      $(attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y))_(
      attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)
      )$
      can be extended to a character $phi$ of $H_z$ such that
      $ phi(z)=(attach(lambda, tl: x))(z^2)=(attach(lambda, tl: y))(z^2). $
      Then
      $
        ((attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y))_(
          attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)
          ))^(H_z)
        =phi+epsilon phi,
      $
      where $epsilon$ is the unique nontrivial character of the factor group
      $H_z slash (attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y))$. The
      #source(348)
      characters $mu(d)^+$ and $mu(d)^-$ are defined as
      $
        mu(d)^+="ind"_(H_z)^G phi,
        mu(d)^-="ind"_(H_z)^G epsilon phi.
      $],
  )
] <th:mackey-induced-linear-character-squares>

We make a few remarks on the proof, which is simply a translation of
Theorem~@th:mackey-symmetric-skew-induced-squares to this more concrete
situation. For a linear character $lambda$, $lambda or lambda$ can be identified
with $lambda^2$, and $lambda and lambda=0$. Let $Z$ be the linear map (defined
in the proof of @th:mackey-symmetric-skew-induced-squares) which was used to
extend the action of $H_(x,y)$ to $H_(x,y,z)$. For $d in frak(D)_1$, the map $Z$
is a scalar:
$
  Z=lambda(x^(-1)z y) dot lambda(y^(-1)z x)
  =lambda(x^(-1)z^2 x)=(attach(lambda, tl: x))(z^2),
$
because of the linearity of $lambda$. Given the extension $phi$ corresponding to
$Z$, the fact that
$
  [(attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y))_(
    attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)
    )]^(H_z)
  =phi+epsilon phi
$
follows from @cor:gallagher-invariant-character (or from
Lemma~@lem:index-two-extension-induced-splitting).

We can now apply Theorem~@th:mackey-induced-linear-character-squares, together
with Proposition~@prop:invariants-induction-hom(ii), to calculate
$i_s lr((overline(lambda)^G,lambda^G))$ and
$i_a lr((overline(lambda)^G,lambda^G))$ for an induced linear character
$lambda^G$ (as in @th:mackey-induced-linear-character-squares). We require two
facts: first, $overline(lambda)^G$ is the character afforded by the
contragredient character $(lambda^G)^*$ of $lambda^G$, by
@prop:contragredient-induction; second,
$
  i_s lr((overline(lambda)^G,lambda^G))
  =dim_K lr(("inv"_G lambda^G or lambda^G)),
  i_a lr((overline(lambda)^G,lambda^G))
  =dim_K lr(("inv"_G lambda^G and lambda^G)),
$
by definition. Before stating the result, let us define, for $d in frak(D)_2$,
$
  j(d)=cases(
    1 & "if" attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y)=1 "on"
        attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y),
    0 & "otherwise",
  ),
$
where $d=D union D^(-1)$, and $x^(-1)y in D$ as in
@th:mackey-induced-linear-character-squares. Note that $j(d)$ is independent of
the choice of $x$ and $y$. For $d in frak(D)_1$, set $j(d)=0$ if
$attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y)!=1$ on
$attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)$. If
$attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y)=1$ on
$attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)$, then
$(attach(lambda, tl: x))(z^2)=plus.minus 1$, and we set
$j(d)=(attach(lambda, tl: x))(z^2)$, again noting that $j(d)$ is independent of
the choice of $x,y$ and $z$.

#theorem[
  Let $lambda$ be a complex linear character of $H$. Then
  $
    i_s lr((overline(lambda)^G,lambda^G))=i(overline(lambda),lambda)
    +sum_(d in frak(D)_1) frac(1, 2)j(d)(1+j(d))
    +sum_(d in frak(D)_2) j(d),
  $
  and
  $
    i_a lr((overline(lambda)^G,lambda^G))
    =sum_(d in frak(D)_1) frac(1, 2)j(d)(-1+j(d))
    +sum_(d in frak(D)_2) j(d).
  $
] <th:induced-linear-symmetric-intertwining-numbers>

#source(349)
The result is a corollary of the previous theorem, and the proof requires only a
few comments. From Exercise~@exc:character-invariants, we have
$
  i_s lr((overline(lambda)^G,lambda^G))
  =dim_K lr(("inv"_G lambda^G or lambda^G))
  =(lambda^G or lambda^G,1_G).
$
Since $lambda^G or lambda^G$ is a sum of induced characters, by
@th:mackey-induced-linear-character-squares, the scalar product
$(lambda^G or lambda^G,1_G)$ is easily computed using Frobenius Reciprocity. For
example,
$
  ((lambda^2)^G,1_G)=(lambda^2,1_H)
  =(overline(lambda),lambda)=i(overline(lambda),lambda).
$
For $d in frak(D)_1$, we have
$
  (phi^G,1_G)=(phi,1_(H_z))=cases(
    0 & "if" j(d)=-1 "or" 0,
    1 & "if" j(d)=1,
  ),
$
so that $(phi^G,1_G)=frac(1, 2)j(d)(1+j(d))$. Similarly
$
  ((epsilon phi)^G,1_G)=(epsilon phi,1_(H_z))
  =(phi,epsilon)=cases(
    0 & "if" j(d)=1 "or" 0,
    1 & "if" j(d)=-1,
  ),
$
and we have $((epsilon phi)^G,1_G)=frac(1, 2)j(d)(-1+j(d))$. For
$d in frak(D)_2$ we have a contribution
$
  ((attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y))^G,1_G)
  =(attach(lambda, tl: x) dot attach(lambda, tl: y),
    1_(attach(H, tl: x) inter attach(H, tl: y)))=j(d).
$
Similar remarks apply to the computation of
$i_a lr((overline(lambda)^G,lambda^G))$.

#corollary[
  Let $lambda$ be a linear character of a subgroup $H$ of $G$, as in
  @th:mackey-induced-linear-character-squares. Then
  $ c(lambda^G)=c(lambda)+sum_(d in frak(D)_1) j(d). $
] <cor:induced-linear-symmetry-invariant>

In case $lambda=1_H$, we have $j(d)=1$ for all $d in frak(D)_1$, and we obtain a
result due to Frame~@bib:Frame1941:

#corollary[
  We have $c((1_H)^G)="card" frak(D)_1$, where $frak(D)_1$ is the family of all
  self-inverse $(H,H)$-double cosets in $G$, including $H$.
] <cor:frame-self-inverse-double-cosets>

#exercises-heading

#exercise[
  Prove Lemma~@lem:index-two-extension-induced-splitting as follows. There is an
  $R$-exact sequence
  $ 0 -> N^- -> N^X ->^alpha N -> 0, $
  #source(350)
  where
  $ alpha(sum a_i times.o n_i)=sum a_i n_i,a_i in R X,n_i in N, $
  and
  $ N^-=ker alpha={1 times.o n-x times.o x^(-1)n:n in N}. $
  The map
  $ n -> 1 times.o n-x times.o x^(-1)n,n in N, $
  gives an $R U$-isomorphism $N tilde.equiv N^-|_U$. On the other hand, $alpha$
  is split by the $R X$-homomorphism $beta:N -> N^X$, defined by
  $ beta(n)=frac(1, 2)(1 times.o n+x times.o x^(-1)n),n in N. $
  Thus $N^X=N^+ plus.o N^-$, where $N^+="im" beta$. It is easily verified that
  $N^+ tilde.equiv N|_U$.
] <exc:index-two-extension-splitting-proof>

#exercise[
  Let $R$ be a commutative ring, and let $M$ be a free $R$-module with a finite
  basis. Prove that for each $r>=0$, $and^(r)(M)$ is isomorphic to the
  $R$-submodule $tilde(and)^(r)(M)$ of $times.o^r M$ generated by all tensors of
  the form
  $
    sum_(sigma in S_r) epsilon(sigma)(x_(sigma(1)) times.o dots.c
      times.o x_(sigma(r))),x_i in M,
  $
  where each $sigma in S_r$ acts as a symmetry operator on $times.o^r M$. Show
  that $tilde(and)^(r)(M)$ is, in general, properly contained in the submodule
  ${t in times.o^r M:sigma t=epsilon(sigma)t}$ (see
  @prop:symmetric-exterior-tensors-as-invariants).
  #hint([@exc:exterior-alternating-tensor-image])[
    Let ${m_1,dots,m_n}$ be an $R$-basis of $M$ and for each sequence
    ${i_1<i_2<dots<i_r,1<=i_j<=n}$, let
    $
      m_(i_1) tilde(and) dots.c tilde(and) m_(i_r)
      =sum_(sigma in S_r) epsilon(sigma)m_(i_(sigma(1))) times.o dots.c
      times.o m_(i_(sigma(r))).
    $
    Prove that the elements ${m_(i_1) tilde(and) dots.c tilde(and) m_(i_r)}$
    form a basis for the module in question.
  ]
] <exc:exterior-alternating-tensor-image>

#exercise[
  Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero, and let $V$ be a vector space of
  dimension $n$ over $K$, and $V^*$ its $K$-dual. Let ${v_1,dots,v_n}$ be a
  basis of $V$, and let ${x_1,dots,x_n}$ be the dual basis. Prove that the
  symmetric algebra $upright(bold(S))(V^*)$ is isomorphic to the $K$-algebra of
  functions $f:V -> K$ generated by ${x_1,dots,x_n}$, and that ${x_1,dots,x_n}$
  are algebraically independent. We shall identify $upright(bold(S))(V^*)$ with
  the algebra $K[x_1,dots,x_n]$, and call it the _algebra of polynomial
  functions_ on $V$#idx("algebra (over a ring)", "of polynomial functions").
  Note that if
  $
    f=sum a_(i_1 dots.c i_n)x_1^(i_1) dots.c x_n^(i_n)
    in K[x_1,dots,x_n],
  $
  then $f$ acts on $V$ according to the rule
  $
    v -> f(v)=sum a_(i_1 dots.c i_n)
    x_1 lr((v))^(i_1) dots.c x_n lr((v))^(i_n).
  $
] <exc:symmetric-algebra-polynomial-functions>

#source(351)
#exercise[
  Keep the notation of the preceding exercise. Let $K$ be algebraically closed
  and of characteristic zero, let $G$ be a finite group, and $V$ a f.g. left
  $K G$-module. Prove that $G$ acts on the algebra of polynomial functions on
  $V$ according to the rule:
  $ (x f)(v)=f(x^(-1)v),x in G,v in V,f in upright(bold(S))(V^*), $
  and that the resulting operations on $upright(bold(S))(V^*)$ are $K$-algebra
  automorphisms. The set
  $ I(G)={f in upright(bold(S))(V^*):x f=f "for all" x in G} $
  is a $K$-subalgebra of $upright(bold(S))(V^*)$, called the _algebra of
  polynomial invariants_#idx(
    "algebra (over a ring)",
    "of polynomial invariants",
  ) of the $K G$-module $V$.
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [For each $r>=0$, let $S_r$ be the homogeneous component of
      $upright(bold(S))(V^*)$ of degree $r$, that is, $S_r$ is generated by all
      monomials
      $ {x_1^(i_1) dots.c x_n^(i_n):i_1+dots+i_n=r}. $
      Prove that
      $
        I(G)=plus.o_(r>=0) I_r,
        "where" I_r=I(G) inter S_r,r>=0,
      $
      and that each $S_r$ is stable under the action of $G$ on
      $upright(bold(S))(V^*)$.],
    [For each $r>=0$, let $a_r=dim_K I_r$. Prove that
      $ a_r=(theta_r,1_G)=|G|^(-1)sum_(x in G) "Tr"(x,S_r), $
      where $theta_r$ is the character of the $K$-representation of $G$ on
      $S_r$.
      #hint([@exc:polynomial-invariants-molien])[
        Use Exercise~@exc:character-invariants.
      ]],
    [Let $x in G$, and let ${xi_1,dots,xi_n}$ be the eigenvalues of $x$ acting
      on the vector space $V$, counted with multiplicities. Let $t$ be an
      indeterminate over $K$. Prove that
      $ det(1-t x)=product_(i=1)^n (1-t xi_i), $
      where $1-t x$ is viewed as a $K(t)$-endomorphism of $K(t) times.o_K V$.],
    [_Molien's Theorem_#idx("Molien's Theorem"). Keep the notation of parts
      (i)–(iii). Prove that for each $x in G$, $det(1-t x)$ is invertible in the
      ring $K[[t]]$ of formal power series over $K$, and that we have an
      identity in $K[[t]]$,
      $
        sum_(r=0)^infinity a_r t^r
        =|G|^(-1)sum_(x in G) frac(1, det(1-t x)),
      $
      where $sum a_r t^r$ is the generating function of the dimensions
      ${a_r:r>=0}$.
      #source(352)
      #hint([@exc:polynomial-invariants-molien])[
        By part(ii),
        $ a_r=|G|^(-1)sum_(x in G) "Tr"(x,S_r). $
        For each $x in G$, let ${xi_1,dots,xi_n}$ be the eigenvalues of $x^(-1)$
        on $V$. Show that the eigenvalues of $x$ acting on $S_r$ are
        $ {xi_1^(i_1) dots.c xi_n^(i_n):i_1+dots+i_n=r}. $
        Then apply (iii) to prove that
        $
          frac(1, det(1-t x^(-1)))
          =frac(1, (1-t xi_1) dots.c (1-t xi_n))
          =sum_(r=0)^infinity
          (sum_(sum i_j=r) xi_1^(i_1) dots.c xi_n^(i_n))t^r
          =sum_(r=0)^infinity "Tr"(x,S_r)t^r.
        $
        Finally, combine these results and use the fact that inversion permutes
        $G$ to obtain the conclusion of Molien's Theorem.
      ]],
  )
] <exc:polynomial-invariants-molien>

#exercise[
  Let ${psi_m}$ be the Adams operators on $"ch" K G$. Consider the character
  tables at the end of §@ss:character-table, and also the
  table~@eq:a5-character-table. For each irreducible character $zeta$ in these
  tables, calculate $psi_2 lr((zeta))$ and $psi_3 lr((zeta))$. Then use
  @eq:adams-newton-recursion to find the characters of $G$ afforded by the
  exterior powers $and^2(Z_i)$ and $and^3(Z_i)$, where $Z_i$ ranges over a basic
  set of simple $K G$-modules.
] <exc:adams-exterior-character-tables>

#exercise[
  Let $bold(X)={X_1,dots,X_n}$ be a set of $n$ variables, and let
  $
    q_1 lr((bold(X)))=X_1+dots+X_n,
    q_2 lr((bold(X)))=sum_(i<=j) X_i X_j,
    q_3 lr((bold(X)))=sum_(i<=j<=k) X_i X_j X_k,
  $
  and so on. Then
  $
    g(t)=product_(j=1)^n (1+t X_j+t^2 X_j^2+dots)
    =1+q_1 t+q_2 t^2+dots.
  $
  Prove that
  $
    q_1+2q_2 t+3q_3 t^2+dots
    ={p_1+p_2 t+p_3 t^2+dots}{1+q_1 t+q_2 t^2+dots},
  $
  where the ${p_i}$ are the power sums occurring in
  @eq:newton-power-sum-identities. Find the identities, analogous to those in
  @eq:newton-power-sum-identities, which connect the ${q_i}$ and ${p_i}$.
] <exc:complete-symmetric-power-sum-identities>

#exercise[
  Let $M$ be a $K G$-module affording the character $mu$, and let ${psi_m}$ be
  the Adams operators on $"ch" K G$. Using the results of the preceding
  Exercise, show how to calculate the characters afforded by the symmetric
  powers of $M$, in terms of the virtual characters
  ${psi_m lr((mu)):m=1,2,dots}$. Determine the characters of
  $upright(bold(S))^2(Z_i)$ and $upright(bold(S))^3(Z_i)$ explicitly, where the
  ${Z_i}$ are as in Exercise~@exc:adams-exterior-character-tables.
] <exc:adams-symmetric-character-powers>
